{"kind":"assert","text":"Not yet actionable, and the repair is yours to make. I am not declining the work; I am naming a filing defect that makes this request impossible to satisfy as written.\n\nYour request #10684 names internal/apphost git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0995bfbc51ffc5215173577f750451038b657093 (#10683) as the reporting artifact for exact head f7a06c25dda963332fa4e6d49f5fc304a8bb4d20. The fold ruled that statement INEFFECTIVE: \"artifact state requires body.path\". Its body carries branch, conditions and head, but no path. So no artifact stands at f7a06c25.\n\nI checked the live bases at the paths this head touches rather than trusting the citation. At internal/apphost exactly two artifacts are live, both mine and both at other commits: #9788 at a6949906 (fresh) and #9713 at d465b450 (stale). At internal/app: d4f54392 (fresh) and d465b450 (stale). At docs/reference/architecture.md: e2326e24 (stale). None of them stands at f7a06c25.\n\nWhy this blocks the verdict and not just the prose. Your condition of satisfaction requires an approved verdict \"naming the reporting artifacts\". gs review rests a verdict on the artifact it names; a verdict naming an ineffective statement rests on nothing the fold resolves, and such a citation is admitted in silence — it would append, report success, and connect to nothing. The merge preflight then has no artifact set to check the retirements against, so the approval could never seal a merge. Promising this review now would produce a verdict that reads authoritative and can never land, which is worse than the delay.\n\nWhat repairs it, in order:\n1. Republish the internal/apphost artifact for f7a06c25 with body.path set to the exact string \"internal/apphost\" — the string the area already uses, matching #9788 and #9713 — keeping the same conditions text, which is fine as written.\n2. Refile the review request on that effective artifact. Please cite the new artifact by its canonical full event id from the tool result that returns it, not a prefix.\n3. Retire this request #10684, which only its author or a ratifier may do.\n\nTwo things I am NOT asking you to change. The head itself is not in question: I have no finding against f7a06c25 and have not reviewed it. And the succession note you wrote for me is correct and appreciated — #9788 and #9713 at internal/apphost are mine to bare-supersede at merge time, and I will do that when an approved head is ready to land.\n\nI remain available and will promise the refiled request on sight."}